+### Key findings (requests, shared bottlenecks)
+
+#### 1. `TriggerWebhookWorker` inline execution (~8-12% wall, shared by all Fabricate-heavy specs)
+
+- Same mechanism as models: `Fabricate(:account)` → `Fabricate(:user)` → `User#trigger_webhooks` → `TriggerWebhookWorker.perform_async('account.created', ...)`; `Fabricate(:status)` → `'status.created'`. With `:inline`, each executes `WebhookService#call` → `serialize_event` → AMS + JSON.
+- StackProf (load-excluded):
+  - `accounts_spec`: `TriggerWebhookWorker#perform` 12.0% total, `WebhookService#call` 11.0%, AMS `instrument_rendering` 10.5%.
+  - `notifications_spec` (with load): webhook 11.0%, `WebhookService` 10.4%.
+  - `favourites_spec` (light): webhook 6.7%, AMS 6.4%.
+- Lower than models (23-33%) because requests also have Distribution/Notify/Mailer costs diluting the share, but still significant and shared (971 `Fabricate` calls in requests, 198 `account/user/status`).
+- Spot-test stub alone (2-run avg):
+  - accounts 3.25→2.75 (-11.8%), notifications 7.90→6.81 (-16.3% in early single-run; stable -15-16%), statuses 4.81→3.76? (combined, see below), home 1.96→1.68 (-12.9%).
+- Safety: No request spec asserts webhook delivery. `grep -rn TriggerWebhook|have_enqueued.*Webhook spec/requests` → only `admin/webhooks_spec.rb` (CRUD, not delivery). Specs asserting other workers (`BootstrapTimelineWorker`, `UpdateDistributionWorker`, `RemovalWorker`, `Fasp::*`, etc.) explicitly set `Sidekiq.testing!(:fake)` then `have_enqueued_sidekiq_job` — unaffected by webhook-only stub. `activitypub/inboxes_spec` stubs `FollowersSynchronizationWorker` locally with `allow(...).to receive` + `have_received` — compatible with global webhook stub (different worker).
+
+#### 2. `ActiveRecord` SQL logging (~7-9% wall, shared)
+
+- `ActiveRecord::LogSubscriber#sql` total 6.35% (load-excluded, accounts), `BroadcastLogger` total 19% (includes AR + Cache + ActionController + Chewy). `IO#write` self 1.79% (file writes to `log/test.log`).
+- Log size check for `accounts_spec` (39 ex): 1.6M, 7242 lines baseline (2491 SQL, 1065 Cache, 149 Started/Processing/Completed, 78 Chewy). With `AR.logger=nil`: 336K, 4228 lines, 33 SQL (load only), time 3.21→3.04 (-5% single run, -7-9% avg).
+- With `Rails.logger.level=FATAL` (silences all, same BroadcastLogger object): 8.9K, 42 lines, time 3.25→2.82 (-13% single run) vs AR-only 2.89 (-11%). Extra 2% from Cache/Chewy silencing is within variance and risks breaking future `Rails.logger` assertions. Models precedent leaves `Rails.logger` intact (for `remotable_spec`); requests has no `Rails.logger` assertions (`grep -rn Rails.logger spec/requests` → none), but keep same `AR.logger=nil` approach for consistency and safety.
+- Spot-test AR-nil alone:
+  - accounts 3.25→2.89 (-7.4%), notifications 7.90→7.44 (-8.6%), statuses 4.81→4.27 (-9.5%), home 1.96→1.76 (-8.8%).
+- Combined webhook+AR (2-run avg): accounts 2.74 (-15.7%), notifications 6.31 (-20.1%), statuses 3.76 (-21.8%), home 1.70 (-13.3%). Total 17.92→14.51s (-19%) for 4 files. Need ~10% more for 30%.
+
+#### 3. `NotifyService#send_email!` via `deliver_later` inline (~13-19% in notification-heavy specs, zero in others, but safe globally)
+
+- Chain: `PostStatusService`/`FollowService`/`ReblogService`/`FavouriteService` in `before` blocks → `DistributionWorker` inline → `FanOutOnWriteService` → `LocalNotificationWorker` inline → `NotifyService#call` → `send_email!` → `NotificationMailer.with(...).type.deliver_later(wait: 2.minutes)` → Sidekiq inline ignores `wait`/`at` (see `Sidekiq::TestingClient#atomic_push` → `process_job` without `at` check) → `MailDeliveryJob#perform` → `deliver_now` → Premailer + rendering + `mail.deliver`.
+- StackProf `notifications_spec`: `LocalNotificationWorker#perform` 22.4%, `NotifyService#call` 21.5%, `DistributionWorker#perform` 17.6%, `FanOutOnWriteService#call` 17.2%, `NotifyService#send_email!` 13.6%, `ActionMailer::MessageDelivery#deliver_later` 13.6%, `MailDeliveryJob#perform` 13.0%, `deliver_now` 13.0%.
+- Only 8 files use `PostStatusService`/`FollowService` directly (`notifications_spec` v1+v2, `conversations_spec`, `follow_requests_spec`, `timelines/home|list|tag_spec`, `statuses/favourites|reblogs_spec`), but those are among the heaviest (notifications 7.9s, timelines 10.78s for 43 ex, etc.). Stubbing email globally helps heavy files a lot without hurting light files (no-op when no notifications).
+- Spot-test `allow_any_instance_of(NotifyService).to receive(:send_email!)` alone:
+  - notifications 7.90→6.38 (-19.2%), accounts 3.25→3.11 (-4.3%), home 1.96→2.06 (+5% overhead, within variance, due to `allow_any_instance` cost when no emails).
+- Combined webhook+AR+email (prepend, no RSpec mock overhead): notifications 7.90→4.79 (-39.4%), accounts 3.25→2.60 (-20%), conversations 2.11→1.59 (-24.6%), follow_requests 2.18→1.42 (-34.9%), statuses 4.81→3.69 (-23.3%). 12 files total 33.03→24.16s (-26.9%).
+- Safety: No request spec asserts `NotificationMailer`. `grep -rn NotificationMailer spec/requests` → none. `capture_emails` in requests only for `AdminMailer` (account_actions, reports) and empty-check (admin confirmations) — different code paths (`Admin::AccountAction`, `ReportService`, not `NotifyService`). Service specs (`notify_service_spec`) test `NotifyService` directly with `capture_emails` — our stub checks `metadata[:type]==:request`, so service specs (type `:service`) still call `super` and pass (verified: 61 ex, 0 failures).
+- Implementation choice: prepend module with `RSpec.current_example` metadata check, not `allow_any_instance_of`, to avoid per-test mock overhead for files with no emails. Prepend once in `before(:suite)`; check is cheap (thread-local + hash lookup) vs mailer cost (rendering + Sidekiq + file I/O).
+
+#### 4. Minor / shared but small
+
+- `redis.del(redis.keys)` + `Rails.cache.clear` after each: <1ms per test as in models. `flushdb` equivalent on dedicated test DB (DB 1), faster/atomic. Extended to requests for consistency.
+- `Sidekiq::Worker.clear_all` after each request: prevents fake-queue pollution between tests that explicitly use `:fake` (e.g., `interaction_policies_spec` with `to_not have_enqueued`, `confirmations_spec` with `to_not`). Clearing after inline tests is no-op. Negligible cost, safety win.
+- `Faker` 4-7% total (e.g., `favourites_spec` 7.4%), `I18n` similar — factory behavior, deferred to factories phase per instructions.
+- `BCrypt` 1.95% self (accounts) — already minimal (`stretches 1` in test), not worth touching.
+- `PG exec` 12.4% self (accounts) — necessary DB work.
+- `GC marking` 11% self — reduced indirectly by webhook/email stubs (fewer AMS objects, fewer mail objects).
+
+### Investigated but NOT applied (low value / risky / out of scope)
